  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert $e^{st}$ with $s=j\\omega$ into $e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)$ to interpret pure complex exponentials as constant-magnitude rotating phasors rather than growing or decaying signals.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Generalized exponential signals use $e^{st}$. Here $s$ can carry both growth or decay and oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the pure complex case, set $s=j\\omega$. The real part is zero, so the exponential envelope disappears. Use the concrete base case $e^{jt}$ first so the learner sees a unit-magnitude rotating signal before returning to general $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply Euler's formula to rewrite the signal in rectangular form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This rewrite is a point on the unit circle: the cosine gives the real part, the sine gives the imaginary part, and the angle changes with time while the magnitude stays fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|e^{j\\omega t}|=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $e^{j\\omega t}$ is the pure complex exponential case: it rotates like a phasor, but it does not grow or decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting $s=j\\omega$ in the Generalized Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Starting from $e^{st}$, substitute $s=j\\omega$ and rewrite the signal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the generalized exponential $e^{st}$ with a fixed complex exponent. Here the move is to set $s=j\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That substitution locks the real part of $s$ at zero, so the growth-or-decay envelope disappears and only the oscillatory part remains.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{st}=e^{(j\\omega)t}=e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal becomes $e^{j\\omega t}$. It is still oscillatory, but it is no longer growing or decaying.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Expanding $e^{j\\omega t}$ with Euler's Formula",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Expand $e^{j\\omega t}$ using Euler's formula and identify its real and imaginary parts.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pure complex exponential $e^{j\\omega t}$. To expose its structure, rewrite it with Euler's formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From this form, the real part is $\\cos(\\omega t)$ and the imaginary part is $\\sin(\\omega t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the direct cosine-sine decomposition of $e^{j\\omega t}$, and it is the form you will reuse in the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $e^{j\\omega t}$ as a Rotating Phasor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "image-needed",
        "image_slots": [
          {
            "image_slot_id": "section-001",
            "placement": "section",
            "location_placeholder": "[IMAGE-LOCATION:section-001]",
            "description_placeholder": "[IMAGE-DESCRIPTION:section-001]",
            "image_description": "Textbook-style line art on a white background: draw the complex plane with a labeled real axis and imaginary axis, a unit circle centered at the origin, and a bold phasor arrow from the origin to a point on the circle at angle $\\omega t$ measured counterclockwise from the positive real axis. Label the angle arc $\\omega t$ and keep the arrow length equal to 1. Optionally include a faint second arrow or trail showing an earlier position to suggest counterclockwise rotation as $t$ increases when $\\omega>0$."
          }
        ],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Use the complex-plane picture to explain how $e^{j\\omega t}$ traces a rotating phasor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pair $\\cos(\\omega t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega t)$ from the Euler expansion. Put that pair on the complex plane as a point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "image",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": "section-001"
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\big(\\Re\\{e^{j\\omega t}\\},\\Im\\{e^{j\\omega t}\\}\\big)=\\big(\\cos(\\omega t),\\sin(\\omega t)\\big),\\qquad |e^{j\\omega t}|=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The point stays on the unit circle because its magnitude is $1$. Its angle from the positive real axis is $\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $\\omega>0$, increasing $t$ moves the point counterclockwise. That moving unit-length vector is the phasor interpretation of $e^{j\\omega t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Pure Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Constant Magnitude Instead of Growth or Decay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $e^{j\\omega t}$ with $e^{\\sigma t}$ and decide whether the signal grows, decays, or stays at constant magnitude.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the exponent: $e^{j\\omega t}$ has real part $0$. Only a nonzero real part creates the envelope $e^{\\sigma t}$ that makes a signal grow or decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{\\sigma t},\\qquad \\sigma=0\\Rightarrow \\left|e^{j\\omega t}\\right|=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\sigma=0$, the magnitude stays at $1$ for all $t$. There is no growth and no decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct classification is constant magnitude. Pure complex exponentials are the zero-growth, zero-decay case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
